{
  "id": "Compound::DB01291 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Pirbuterol, as an Adrenergic Beta2-agonist, is linked to Asthma through a series of biological pathways that involve the activation of beta-2 receptors, leading to bronchodilation. The evidence suggests that Pirbuterol, through its inclusion in the broader class of Adrenergic Beta2-agonists, is indirectly associated with Asthma treatment. This is supported by the fact that multiple members of the Adrenergic Beta2-agonist family, such as Indacaterol and Salmeterol, are also linked to Asthma treatment, reinforcing the notion that these compounds share a common mechanism of action. The lowest common ancestors of Pirbuterol and these other agonists are both Organic Cyclic Compounds and Bronchodilators, indicating a shared structural and functional profile. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of beta-2 receptor activation in bronchodilation, which is a key mechanism in managing asthma. The evidence suggests that Pirbuterol may be repurposed for asthma treatment due to its pharmacological profile and its association with bronchodilatory effects, which are central to asthma management.",
